MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

LHC becomes configurable and inspectable. Behind GROK_LHC (still off by
default). Nine core touchpoints; sentinel remains 6/6 — no seventh runtime hook.
151 lib + 85 certification + 5 golden tests.

- [lhc] config section following the host's sectioned pattern, precedence
  env > config.toml > default(off). apply_resolved_config fills only UNSET env
  vars, so existing GROK_LHC* usage is unchanged, and default-sourced values are
  never written to the environment (they used to leak into every child process
  AND make /lhc misreport its own provenance as "source: env").
- /lhc status names the ACTIVE CONTEXT ENGINE from the last serve turn, not from
  capture registration — including the fail-open reason when native built the
  request. The Phase 3 done-definition requires this never be ambiguous; deriving
  it from registration asserted the wrong engine and called it healthy.
- Health, repair and migration: repair confirm binds to a previously displayed
  plan; schema validation reads a 16-byte header rather than fs::read on a
  database designed to hold full history; RPC timeout/error folds into health.ok
  so a stuck worker cannot report healthy.
- Per-session /lhc on|off. Mid-session enable reconstructs SamplerConfig and
  registers the sampler, so compaction genuinely works rather than being
  documented as unavailable.

Five rounds. Every round found a real product defect; none were style. The
sequence is worth reading because each fix caused the next finding:

1. /lhc on could not work at all. The tee is installed once at session spawn and
   owned immutably by the actor, so enabling mid-session registered a worker
   nothing fed. Worse than lost capture: hook 4 gates on registry presence, so
   LHC still SERVED, building requests from a log frozen at bootstrap while the
   conversation moved on. Fail-open could not catch it — a stale log is
   internally consistent.
2. Fixing that required installing the tee unconditionally, so a disabled
   session now costs work per persist. I ruled to keep it (removing it makes A5
   per-session enable structurally impossible) and required a measurement.
3. The measurement was noise — sequential samples, saturating_sub, five runs
   giving 0,0,0,0,7 ns — and it could not observe the case that was broken:
   any_capture_active() is process-wide while the tee is per-session, so a
   disabled session took the GLOBAL REGISTRY MUTEX whenever any other session
   was active. Chunk 2's L3 defect, back in the multisession case. Replaced the
   timing with a counter assertion, which cannot be noise.
4. The generation-cached fix then had a TOCTOU race: lookup under the mutex,
   release, read generation, stamp. A registration in that window pinned a stale
   None with the new generation forever. BOTH verifier lanes found this
   independently in separate trees — the first genuinely independent convergence
   of the project — and both proved it empirically rather than by argument.
5. The fix was correct but its test could not fail: it toggled a racy branch
   inside the helper, so reverting the CALL SITE to the two-observation assembly
   left all 151 tests green. I found this by breaking it myself.

Round 5 closed it by making the defect unwritable rather than merely detected:
RegistrySnapshot has private fields and is stamped only under the registry
mutex. Verifiers attacked that three ways — direct construction (E0451),
post-hoc desync of a legitimate snapshot (E0616), and reaching for the test-only
constructor from production (E0599, with the same source compiling under
test-util). All three blocked. The claim's honest boundary: field privacy is
module-scoped, so the defect is unwritable from tee.rs, where it lived, not from
inside capture.rs, which owns the invariant.

Both lanes PASS with no blocking or carryable findings, in separate trees, each
having independently re-demonstrated the round-4 vacuity gap is closed.

patches/ regeneration list verified to equal
`git diff --name-only origin/main -- crates/codegen/ Cargo.toml` exactly — all
fourteen paths. That invariant silently failed twice before.

Carried to 3B: unknown /lhc subcommands falling through to Status, repair
confirm case-sensitivity, the status early-return asymmetry,
refresh_settings_and_reapply's set_var under live tokio, G2, and Replace
recoverability.

